{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind Adventure in Japan**\n\nJapan dazzled my senses from the first moment. Tokyo’s neon-lit streets hummed with energy—sushi bars tucked between salarymen, vending machines spilling cold drinks, and the occasional koi pond glimmering under skyscrapers. I lost hours in Akihabara, where anime merch and ramen stalls blended into a cyberpunk dream. By night, the Tokyo Skytree stretched into the indigo sky, its lights twinkling like stars.\n\nLeave the city, and tradition unfolds. Kyoto’s bamboo forest whispered secrets as sunlight filtered through towering stalks. Temples like Kinkaku-ji gleamed gold against cherry blossoms—pink petals drifting like confetti. A tea ceremony in a matcha garden taught me patience: steeping, sipping, silence. In Osaka, takoyaki sizzled on street stalls, crispy dough cups stuffed with octopus, a salty-sweet explosion.\n\nThe bullet train whisked me to Hiroshima in 90 minutes. The Peace Memorial Park offered quiet reflection before moving on to Miyajima’s floating torii gate, framed by Mount Misen. Nighttime illuminations turned shrines into fairy tales.\n\nFood became its own adventure: ramen in Tokyo, okonomiyaki in Osaka, kaiseki in Kyoto. Every bowl, every bite, felt like a hug from strangers—generous, precise, and warm.\n\nJapan is a paradox: futuristic and ancient, loud and serene, all in one. It’s a country where ancient tea bowls sit beside robot restaurants, where neon signs fade into misty mountain trails at dawn. I left with my heart full and my camera rolls empty—some moments don’t need capturing. Just living.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.06550939381122589}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.30262133479118347}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.04418794438242912}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.012623289600014687}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -7.652943895664066e-05}, {"token": " Advent